01:59:47,199 --> 01:59:52,719
an MRI frame. These are functions of two

2947
01:59:49,920 --> 01:59:56,080
variables, not just one.

2948
01:59:52,719 --> 01:59:58,320
So, does the Forier series still apply?

2949
01:59:56,080 --> 02:00:00,159
Absolutely. In fact, the transition to

2950
01:59:58,320 --> 02:00:02,159
two dimensions is very smooth and

2951
02:00:00,159 --> 02:00:04,400
intuitive.

2952
02:00:02,159 --> 02:00:06,880
Let's start with the formula at the top.

2953
02:00:04,400 --> 02:00:09,679
This is the 2D Forier transform or more

2954
02:00:06,880 --> 02:00:11,520
precisely the 2D discrete forier series.

2955
02:00:09,679 --> 02:00:14,800
It converts a spatial domain function

2956
02:00:11,520 --> 02:00:17,280
into a frequency domain representation.

2957
02:00:14,800 --> 02:00:19,520
We begin with a function f of m andn.

2958
02:00:17,280 --> 02:00:22,960
Here m andn are the spatial coordinates

2959
02:00:19,520 --> 02:00:25,599
like pixel row and column indices.

2960
02:00:22,960 --> 02:00:27,040
Then we compute the capital f of x and y

2961
02:00:25,599 --> 02:00:29,440
which is the frequency domain

2962
02:00:27,040 --> 02:00:32,400
representation of the image. We get it

2963
02:00:29,440 --> 02:00:37,280
using a double summation from m= 0 to m

2964
02:00:32,400 --> 02:00:39,520
minus1 and from n= 0 to n minus one.

2965
02:00:37,280 --> 02:00:42,400
Each term in the sum includes f of m and

2966
02:00:39,520 --> 02:00:49,360
n multiplied by a complex exponential e

2967
02:00:42,400 --> 02:00:51,440
to ^j 2<unk>i * x * m / m + y * n /n.

2968
02:00:49,360 --> 02:00:54,239
This is just the 2D extension of what we

2969
02:00:51,440 --> 02:00:57,679
did before in 1D. Now applied along both

2970
02:00:54,239 --> 02:00:59,199
spatial directions.

2971
02:00:57,679 --> 02:01:01,679
Next, let's look at the inverse

2972
02:00:59,199 --> 02:01:04,000
transform. The formula at the bottom

2973
02:01:01,679 --> 02:01:06,080
here we reconstruct the original image f

2974
02:01:04,000 --> 02:01:08,719
of m and n from its frequency components

2975
02:01:06,080 --> 02:01:11,199
f of x and y. Again we use a double

2976
02:01:08,719 --> 02:01:13,199
summation ranging from 0 to n minus1 and

2977
02:01:11,199 --> 02:01:15,599
n minus one.

2978
02:01:13,199 --> 02:01:19,040
And we add a normalization factor of 1 /

2979
02:01:15,599 --> 02:01:24,159
m * n. The exponential now becomes e^

2980
02:01:19,040 --> 02:01:26,080
positive j 2 pi * x * m + y * n /n.

2981
02:01:24,159 --> 02:01:28,320
So once again, we're projecting onto a

2982
02:01:26,080 --> 02:01:30,719
set of complex exponential basis

2983
02:01:28,320 --> 02:01:33,920
functions just like in the 1D case, but

2984
02:01:30,719 --> 02:01:36,000
now expanded to two dimensions.

2985
02:01:33,920 --> 02:01:37,840
This idea even extends naturally to

2986
02:01:36,000 --> 02:01:40,880
three dimensions, which is useful for

2987
02:01:37,840 --> 02:01:43,280
things like volutric imaging, 3D scans

2988
02:01:40,880 --> 02:01:45,040
or dynamic time sequence data in medical

2989
02:01:43,280 --> 02:01:47,760
imaging.

2990
02:01:45,040 --> 02:01:50,159
So to summarize, the 2D4A series gives

2991
02:01:47,760 --> 02:01:52,639
us a powerful way to analyze images in

2992
02:01:50,159 --> 02:01:54,719
terms of spatial frequencies. It's

2993
02:01:52,639 --> 02:01:56,080
conceptually the same as the 1D case

2994
02:01:54,719 --> 02:01:59,080
just applied in two directions

2995
02:01:56,080 --> 02:01:59,080
simultaneously.

2996
